ok let's get into audience questions for  this week first one is from Sally hey  David what warning signs do you notice  that someone might be bad with money  okay I've got some macro montt ones and  some micro ones so as a big thing when I  hear that someone is rolling their  existing car loan into their next car  loan because they owe more money than  the car is worth but they really want to  get a new car that's a big red flag  you're paying interest upon interest  you're paying for two cars at once even  though you only have one and that's a  really big red flag to me that someone  might not be that good with their  finances now of course we can all  imagine a situation where you have  someone who has no choice their car has  been has some major repair that's  necessary the cars worth 4 grand they  owe 6 and the repair is 3 grand  does it really make sense to put $3,000  into a $4,000 car so they go and they  trade the car in for 2500 bucks and roll  some of that loan into some new loan in  very specific circumstances I can  understand why that ends up being the  least bad option I'm not talking about  that I have many friends who over the  years particularly in our 20s would say  I'm just sick of this car but I found a  great deal on this one and they're  willing to get me out of my loan and I  say well when you say get you out of the  loan what do you mean by that they say  well they'll pay off the 5 grand that I  owe so oh and how much are you getting  for the trade and they say $3,500 which  basically means you're taking $1,500 in  negative equity and adding it to your  next car loan and that's just going to  compound so that's rarely a good option  and it's a sign to me that people are  not good in terms of personal finance  another one is anytime I ask someone how  much something costs and they tell me  how much it costs per month that's not a  good sign now with something like a car  it's a little bit different because very  often you are to thinking in terms of a  monthly cost but if I ask someone how  much is one of these TVs and they say  it's only 90 bucks a month bad that's a  really really bad sign you should not be  going into debt to pay for a big-screen  TV with a car yes most people are paying  for that monthly there's a strong case  to be made that you should not go into  debt for a car that you should find the  cheapest possible car  if it's $900 or $1,500 and run it into  the ground but when I say how much is  this and someone gives me a monthly  number that's a red flag for me what if  it's only 12 easy payments of a monthly  number that people used to work at  Circuit City and actually later I'm in  the show I'm going to talk about that  and one of the things that was used was  hey you can buy this over 12 months with  no interest and people fell for it  because they said well if it's no  interest then I'm paying the same amount  over 12 months than if I just paid for  it now the difference being that  psychologically you're willing to buy a  much more expensive thing if you know  that you're only making 12 smaller  payments instead of one lump sum so even  without interest it's still a mechanism  that people can get tricked by another  one is when a friend says my credit card  debt is crushing me and then I noticed  that they go to a coffee shop everyday  and they get a $6 frappe a caramel  whatever type of thing and I'm not one  of these latte Millionaire dudes who  talks about if you never bought a coffee  then in 15 years  all that money compounded at 7% would  make you a millionaire that's not what  I'm saying  but if you are spending 6 bucks on an  expensive coffee drink or a coffee and a  croissant every single day but you're  complaining about credit card debt  that's 6 bucks a day should be going  directly to your credit card in order to  not pay interest on that and the same  thing applies for drinks like sometimes  I'll go out with friends and I have a  sense of what people are making from 